{"v":1,"accession":"PRJNA1521666","title":"Enhancing sewer sediment permeability via micro-nano bubble-mediated structural remodeling and EPS reduction","description":"Sewer sediments contain dense biofilms enriched in extracellular polymeric substances EPS which severely impede chemical infiltration and pose a major obstacle to sediment control. Herein micro-nano bubbles (MNBs) are employed for the first time to realize three dimensional structural remodeling and EPS depletion of sediments thereby enhancing their permeability. The three-dimensional analysis revealed that sewer sediments consist of cluster biofilms ( 0.78 mm) and bridging biofilms (0.12 mm), forming a dense gel network with a low pore content. Results showed that at 20 % MNBs addition the complete tracer penetration time decreased from 36 h to 8 h and the migration speed was 3.5 times higher than the control group. X-ray computed tomography analysis revealed that MNBs reduced tortuosity from 1.754 to 1.352 increased pore volume fraction from 0.083 to 0.105 and reduced clustered biofilm count from 280 to 178. EPS analysis indicated significant reductions with protein content decreasing from 5.6 to 1.4 mg/g VSS and polysaccharides from 3.8 to 1.9 mg/g VSS at depth. ORP increased from -280 mV to -88 mV shifting the microbial community from anaerobic to aerobic dominance. The mechanism involved synergistic effects between structural remodeling and EPS reduction providing theoretical support for MNBs application in urban sewage pipe maintenance.","center":"Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","submission_date":"2026/09/01 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMN62808419","sediment","probable_urban","municipal_wast